  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 AM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over far northern Alberta,\nwith high over the Midwest into the Southern Plains. Upper level\nanalysis places ridge over the Central Rockies stretching\nnorthward to the lee of the Canadian Rockies, while a notable\nshort wave is now moving onshore over British Columbia. Over our\narea, quiet weather continues with a light south to\nsouthwesterly breeze between the two surface features. \n\nFor today, upper ridge pushes over our area while aforementioned\nshort wave slides over the Canadian Rockies and into the\nCanadian Prairies by early evening. In response, surface low\nshifts over Saskatchewan by early evening and deepens. This will\nbring southwesterly winds around 15 mph to our area, with high\ntemperatures pushing into the mid 60s to mid 70s, about 10 to 20\ndegrees above average. Over far southwest North Dakota, relative\nhumidity values are expected to drop into the teens, which will\ncombine with the southwesterly wind to bring near critical fire\nweather conditions. \n\nOvernight, short wave will continue its easterly push through\nthe prairie provinces, flattening the upper ridge. Surface low\nwill slide over Manitoba, while a cold front drops through our\narea late night, shifting winds northwesterly in its wake.\n\nOn Sunday, surface low pushes over Hudson Bay while cold front\ncontinues its push through far south central North Dakota and\nthe James River Valley in the morning. Although dry conditions\nwill continue, temperatures will be cooler but still near to a\nbit above average. \n\nZonal flow remains in place to start the work week, with flow\nturning southwesterly as we approach mid-week as a Pacific low \ndrops down along the West Coast. Conditions remain dry, with a \nvery gradual warming trend over our area. Ensemble guidance \nthen suggests that the low shifts easterly and opens up late \nweek as it pushes over the Rockies nudging into the plains. \nThough the timing of this continues to be a bit variable, it \ndoes bring widespread precipitation chances to the forecast \nlate week.",
